Interstitial Cystitis (Bladder Pain Syndrome)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Suprapubic pain worsening with bladder filling, Profound relief following micturition, Debilitating urinary frequency/urgency, Glomerulations/Hunner's lesions on cystoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-029
Reason for Visit: Excruciating, continuous suprapubic pain and an absolute inability to leave the toilet due to agonizing urinary urgency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, escalating pelvic pain and debilitating urinary urgency, rendering the patient effectively housebound and in acute distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively assess for acute pyelonephritis or severe acute bacterial cystitis via clinical examination and urinalysis, and to immediately implement potent analgesic therapy prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My bladder feels like it's full of battery acid and razor blades, the pressure is agonizing, but when I go, it's only drops."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe flare of chronic, continuous suprapubic and pelvic pain. The pain is characterized by a severe exacerbation directly correlated with progressive bladder filling, followed by profound, immediate relief of the pain immediately following micturition. The patient reports debilitating urinary urgency and profound urinary frequency, voiding over 30 times today. Severe nocturia has resulted in chronic sleep deprivation over the last 48 hours. The patient explicitly denies high fevers, rigors, flank pain, or gross hematuria. Symptoms flared following heavy coffee consumption today.
Functional Status: Confined entirely to the bathroom due to the relentless urgency and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound exhaustion and acute distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, guarding the lower abdomen, unable to sit still.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals severe, exquisite tenderness strictly localized to the suprapubic region (directly over the bladder). Complete absence of generalized peritonism, guarding, or rigidity in the upper quadrants. Renal angle (costovertebral) percussion is completely negative bilaterally. Urinalysis via dipstick is completely normal, lacking leukocytes, nitrites, or gross blood. Documented history of a normal urinalysis lacking infection combined with visualization of petechial haemorrhages (glomerulations) on a prior formal cystoscopy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty coffee cups on the counter, heavily correlating with the acute dietary trigger.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; using over-the-counter paracetamol with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Interstitial Cystitis (Bladder Pain Syndrome) - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Cystitis (UTI) - Ruled out immediately by the completely normal dipstick urinalysis lacking leukocytes/nitrites, and absence of fever.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out by the complete absence of fever, rigors, and negative costovertebral angle tenderness.

Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stone) - Ruled out by the clinical pattern (pain strictly related to bladder filling/emptying rather than colicky flank pain radiating to the groin).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to agonizing, severe pelvic pain and functional incapacitation (confined to the toilet) mimicking a severe acute genitourinary infection. The intensity of the pain and the extreme urgency mandated an immediate clinical evaluation and urinalysis to definitively rule out acute bacterial cystitis or pyelonephritis. By visually confirming a strictly negative urinalysis and localizing the pain functionally to bladder distension, the physician safely diagnosed a severe inflammatory flare. This immediate exclusion of an infectious emergency safely prevented an inappropriate emergency department admission and avoided the unnecessary prescription of broad-spectrum antibiotics, fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for localized anti-inflammatory effect. Advised immediate, total cessation of all caffeine, acidic foods, and alcohol.
Positive Behavioral Change: Dilute the urine aggressively by drinking exactly 500ml of plain water immediately, followed by 250ml every hour, while simultaneously applying a heating pad directly over the suprapubic area. The dilution of the urine removes the chemical irritants burning the damaged bladder lining, while the heat relaxes the spastic bladder muscle, rewarding the patient with a rapid, physical reduction in the severe burning urgency.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 5 days to discuss long-term bladder coating agents (e.g., Elmiron) or neuromodulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever with chills/shaking, sudden passage of bright red blood or clots in the urine, or complete inability to pass any urine despite a full bladder.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes the current normal urinalysis correlates with a lack of active infection; if a fever develops, a formal mid-stream urine culture is mandatory.
Clinical Course: Interstitial cystitis is an inflammatory condition where the protective lining of the bladder is compromised, allowing normal urine to severely burn the underlying nerves. It is intensely painful but structurally benign and responsive to dietary control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever with shaking chills, if you start passing obvious blood, or if you completely stop being able to pee.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bladder wall is inflamed, and the high concentration of caffeine in your urine today acted like acid on an open wound. Antibiotics will not help because there is zero bacteria present. We must dilute the urine to stop the chemical burn.
Actionable Steps: Completely stop all coffee, tea, and citrus juices starting immediately. Drinking only plain water will instantly reduce the acidity of your urine. Experiencing the rapid drop in pain as the urine dilutes will reinforce the absolute necessity of strictly avoiding dietary triggers.

